-- in theory, if I can only render terrain on the client, i can render everything else too, stuff like vehicles will only show if you are within that chunk. resources will be owned individually by players but only live as data on server (waiting to be mined, is exposed, was dropped, no longer existent (this would just be nil of course), etc)
--- chunks will render with terrain first, while that is happening a request to the backend is made to ask for the latest updates on the terrain changes (this is done real-time if they are currently within the chunk; chunked on the server by a simple vector bounds check using the grid position); after those changes are applied (again, requests are happening to the backend as this happens) stuff like vehicles, NPCs, etc will be spawned after (everything is AFTER the terrain data is updated to ensure the most current collision and everything)
--- stuff has a memory on the client, like resource data (whether dropped, last position when dropped and chunk unloaded) will be rendered as the requests are being done, but once the server gives updated info (like if the resource was removed or something) this will then happen
--- vehicles will have their own rendering, so clients will have it, but when someone gets near, these are loaded, stuff like wheels turning is just an event to the server to replicate to clients, and then a fake wheel spin animation is played. other stuff like the drill on a drill car moving or something is also done via events; will have latency but it is what it is. putting everything on the server just isnt feasible, things could be, but since terrain will be the most likely bottleneck, we can't render that therefore we can't render and do other things on the server since basically everything depends on the terrain and it's collision

